summary:
When humanity sets foot on Mars, it is of vital importance to select safe landing zones for our astronauts and cargo vehicles, protect them from radiation, and most importantly, access the nearest water ice resources. However, the data we currently have is extremely scattered and complex.&nbsp; 
The core problem we face lies right here: Existing resources are unfortunately very complex and inadequate. A lack of information or confusion can lead astronauts to face critical risks and serious health hazards.&nbsp; 
To give a more concrete example: The maximum oxygen window for an astronaut during a single surface walk is only 4 hours! Meanwhile, existing tools fall short in planning human footsteps because they remain too large-scale; single-layer surface imagery lacks context, and passive data does not allow for real-time hazard routing.&nbsp; 
That is exactly where we step in with our solution:
First, we adopted a meter-precision approach, tailoring the data strictly to spacesuit limits.&nbsp; 
 Second, we built a multi-layer structure that combines slope, radiation, and minerals on a single screen.&nbsp; 
And most importantly, we integrated an active safety system that provides live oxygen tracking and safe shelter range.
